WEBVTT

00:00:00.157 --> 00:00:03.807
Run the department query and focus first on row grain.

00:00:03.807 --> 00:00:05.712
Each row represents one department.

00:00:05.712 --> 00:00:09.362
The outer aggregate functions summarize admission rows.

00:00:09.362 --> 00:00:16.952
The correlated scalar subquery uses the current department name to count open admissions only for that

00:00:16.952 --> 00:00:22.492
department. This is a useful pattern for dashboards and grouped summaries.

00:00:22.492 --> 00:00:28.062
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.062 --> 00:00:33.207
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:33.207 --> 00:00:36.862
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:36.862 --> 00:00:42.692
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product,

00:00:42.692 --> 00:00:49.292
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:49.292 --> 00:00:52.222
before discussing syntax.

00:00:52.222 --> 00:00:58.000
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
